# Memory layer (workspace dev)

This rule applies when editing the oh-my-cursor repository itself. It
complements the plugin-shipped `rules/memory-and-notepad.mdc` (which applies
to consumer workspaces).

## Hard requirements

- New memory templates live under `docs/templates/`. Do not put runtime
  files in `docs/templates/`; templates are documentation.
- Every new memory surface must have a validator under `scripts/`,
  pytest coverage under `tests/memory/`, and a row in
  `docs/memory-layer.md`.
- Every new template must be listed in the `required` array of
  `scripts/validate-plugin-structure.sh` and included in the rsync payload
  of `scripts/install-local-plugin.sh`.
- The new skills (`remember`, `notepad`, `wiki`, `decisions`,
  `rules-authoring`) must carry the standard governance block: Ownership
  Class, Proof Class, Claim Summary, MCP Integration Points, Hooks
  Dependencies, Orchestration Role.
- Hooks may **read** memory paths for observability (e.g. a future
  compact-reminder mention of notepad) but must not **write** them. This
  is enforced by `scripts/validate-hook-readonly.py`.

## When updating

- If you add or rename a memory template, update `docs/memory-layer.md`,
  `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `docs/confirmed-surfaces.md`, the validator
  list in `scripts/validate-plugin-structure.sh`, and the rsync include
  list in `scripts/install-local-plugin.sh` in the same change.
- If you rename a skill, also update `skills/phase-controller/SKILL.md` and
  `skills/auto-execute/SKILL.md` if they cite it.
- If you change a section cap in `notepad.md`, update the cap in
  `scripts/validate-notepad-format.py` and in the template at
  `docs/templates/notepad.md` together.

## Why this is workspace-dev only

Consumer workspaces should not be forced to follow oh-my-cursor's internal
authoring conventions for memory templates. They get the shipped runtime
rule (`rules/memory-and-notepad.mdc`); this dev rule is just for
maintainers editing the plugin itself, so it lives under `.cursor/rules/`
which the install script explicitly excludes.
